course: geography
lesson: 26
title: Food and farming around the world
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Look at a plate of chips with ketchup and a cup of hot chocolate: the potato, the tomato and the cocoa all came from the Americas, and none of them was eaten in Europe before 1492. Food has a map and a history.
explain: Three grains feed much of humanity: rice, wheat and maize. Rice loves hot, wet river plains with flooded fields; wheat likes cooler, drier lands; and where land is too dry or too cold to farm, people herd animals instead.
explain: After 1492 plants and animals crossed the Atlantic both ways: potatoes, maize and cocoa to the Old World, wheat, horses, cattle and sugar cane to the New. Historians call this the Columbian Exchange.
explain: In Europa Universalis 5 sugar is a valuable trade good from Caribbean colonies and grain is the plain good of many locations. The game shows the price, not the people: each sugar estate was worked by enslaved Africans in brutal conditions, and real farms grew many crops, not one.
explain: Today machines, fertilisers and better seeds let a few farmers feed many, but farming still drinks about 70 percent of the fresh water people take.
fact: Rice, wheat and maize together provide a large share of all the calories people eat in the world.
fact: Rice grows best in hot, wet places with flooded fields (the water also holds back weeds), especially in the river plains and deltas of China, India and South-East Asia.
fact: After 1492 plants and animals crossed the Atlantic in both directions, which historians call the Columbian Exchange.
fact: Potatoes, maize, tomatoes, chillies and cocoa came from the Americas, while wheat, horses, cattle and sugar cane went there; there had been no horses in the Americas before.
fact: The potato was first farmed in the Andes of South America and became a staple food in Europe, from Ireland to Russia, by the 1700s and 1800s.
fact: Many poor Irish families lived almost only on potatoes, so when a disease destroyed Ireland's potatoes in 1845 to 1852, about a million people died in the Great Famine.
fact: Sugar cane plantations in Brazil and the Caribbean were worked mainly by enslaved Africans, in brutal conditions where many died young.
fact: Cattle, sheep, goats and pigs were tamed in South-West Asia, while the llama and alpaca were tamed in the Andes.
fact: On the steppe, dry grassland too dry for crops in many places, and in deserts many peoples lived as nomadic herders, moving with their animals to find fresh grass and water.
fact: Farmers in hilly places cut steps called terraces into the slopes, so the flat steps hold water and stop soil washing away, as in the rice terraces of the Philippines and the farming terraces of the Inca in the Andes.
fact: Today machines, fertilisers and new seed varieties let far fewer farmers grow much more food than in the past, and only about 1 percent of Britons now work on the land.
fact: Farming uses about 70 percent of all the fresh water people take from rivers, lakes and underground; bringing water to fields is called irrigation, and in deserts, with too little rain, crops cannot grow without it.
fact: Coffee came originally from Ethiopia and was first drunk widely in Yemen, while tea came from China.
exercise: Which three grains give a large share of the world's calories? | Rice, wheat and maize | Oats, rye and pearl barley | Rice, potatoes and beans | Wheat, sugar and cocoa
exercise: Where does rice grow best? | In hot, wet places with flooded fields | In cold, dry places with frosty fields | On the tops of high, windy mountains | In sandy deserts without any water
exercise: What do historians call the crossing of plants and animals over the Atlantic after 1492? | The Columbian Exchange | The Silk Road | The Middle Passage | The Great Atlantic Famine
exercise: Which food came from the Americas to Europe? | The tomato | Wheat | Sugar cane | Rice
exercise: Which animal went from Europe to the Americas? | The horse | The llama | The turkey | The alpaca
exercise: Where was the potato first farmed? | In the Andes of South America | In the wet fields of Ireland | In Russia | In the river plains of China
exercise: What happened in Ireland in 1845 to 1852? | Potato disease caused a famine that killed about a million | The first potatoes arrived from America and were planted everywhere | A drought dried up every river | Rice replaced the potato as the main food
exercise: Who mainly worked the sugar plantations of Brazil and the Caribbean? | Enslaved Africans | Paid farmers from Europe | Machines driven by steam | Nomadic herders from the steppe
exercise: Where were cattle, sheep, goats and pigs first tamed? | In South-West Asia | In the Andes of Peru | In northern Australia | In eastern North America
exercise: Which animals were tamed in the Andes? | The llama and alpaca | The horse and the camel | The cow and the farm pig | The sheep and the goat
exercise: How do nomadic herders live? | They move with their animals to find grass and water | They stay in one city all their lives and buy their food at market | They grow rice in flooded fields | They fish in the ocean all year
exercise: What are terraces in farming? | Steps cut into hillsides to make flat fields | Big glass greenhouses built on the tops of hills | Channels that drain a swamp | Fences that keep animals in
exercise: Why can far fewer farmers grow much more food today? | Machines, fertilisers and better seeds | There are fewer people to feed | The weather is better everywhere | Plants grow faster when there are fewer farmers
exercise: About what share of the fresh water people take is used for farming? | About 70 percent | Only about 7 percent | Only about 1 percent | Almost none of it
exercise: Where did coffee come from originally? | Ethiopia | Brazil | Colombia | Italy
exercise: Where did tea come from? | China | India | Britain | Morocco
test: My mate says potatoes are an old Irish food, been there forever. True? | No, they came from the Andes and reached Europe after 1492 | Yes, the potato was first farmed in Ireland | Yes, the Romans brought them to Ireland | No, they came from China along the Silk Road
test: Why did the Inca use llamas, not horses, to carry goods? | There were no horses in the Americas before Europeans brought them | Horses were banned by the Inca king because he was afraid of them | Llamas are much faster than horses | Horses cannot walk on any mountain
test: Why was relying on one crop so dangerous for Ireland? | One disease could wipe out almost all the food | Potatoes give no energy at all, so people grew weak | Potatoes cannot grow in rain | Other crops were illegal in Ireland
test: In the game, sugar is just a pricey trade good from the Caribbean. What does that leave out? | Sugar was grown by enslaved Africans in brutal conditions | Sugar was grown by well-paid workers who chose to sail out from Europe | Sugar grew wild and nobody had to work | Sugar came only from Europe
test: Why do farmers cut terraces into steep slopes? | Flat steps hold water and stop soil washing away | Terraces make the hill grow taller every year | Terraces keep the cold mountain wind off the farmers | Steps are easier to paint
test: Why could the steppe not be farmed like France? | It is too dry, so people herded animals over the grass | The soil there is made of salt | The steppe is covered in thick, dark forests that cannot be cleared | Steppe peoples did not know about plants
test: why do rice farmers flood their fields, seems mental | Rice grows well in water, which also holds back weeds | To stop the rice from getting thirsty in winter | To wash the rice before they eat it | To keep the fish away from the plants
test: Dad says everyone in Britain still works on farms like in the old days. Put him right. | Only about 1 percent do, thanks to machines | He is right, most Britons are still farmers today | Nobody at all works on farms in Britain | About half of Britons work on farms
test: Why does farming in deserts need irrigation? | There is too little rain to water the crops | Desert sun makes plants grow too fast | Desert crops need salt water | Irrigation keeps animals off the fields
test: Name two foods that came from the Americas to Europe. | Maize and chillies | Wheat and sugar cane | Rice and tea leaves | Coffee and black pepper
test: What would a pizza look like without the Columbian Exchange? | It would have no tomato sauce | It would have no bread base | It would have no cheese | It would look exactly the same
test: The port of Mocha in Yemen gave its name to a coffee drink. Why there? | Coffee was first drunk widely in Yemen | Coffee was first grown in Italy | Mocha was where coffee was invented in a factory | Yemen is where tea comes from
exam: Identify the principal consequence of the transfer of plants and animals across the Atlantic subsequent to 1492. | Crops such as the potato and maize entered the diet of the Old World | The cultivation of rice ceased throughout Asia | The horse became extinct in Europe | The trade in all foodstuffs between the continents was thereupon abolished entirely
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises nomadic pastoralism? | The movement of herders with their livestock in search of pasture and water | The cultivation of rice in flooded fields upon fixed estates throughout the year | The construction of terraces upon steep slopes for grain | The purchase of all foodstuffs in permanent urban markets
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the use of fresh water. | Agriculture accounts for approximately seventy per cent of human withdrawals | Agriculture accounts for approximately one per cent of human withdrawals | Domestic washing accounts for the greater part of withdrawals | Fresh water is not employed in agriculture to any degree
exam: It is given that the sugar plantations of Brazil and the Caribbean required great quantities of labour. Ascertain by whom this labour was principally performed. | Enslaved persons forcibly transported from Africa | Salaried agricultural labourers who emigrated from Europe | Steam-driven machinery introduced in 1492 | Nomadic herders from the Asian steppe
